; THE LIBRARY TEST PROGRAMS UNDER THE REAL KERNEL -- task 25c.
;
; boot.ini reaches a shell prompt, console.ini types at it, session.ini has it write files.
; This one has it run the twenty-seven programs of lib/test out of /usr/test, each with both
; descriptors on a file in /tmp, and then calls sync.  Everything that matters afterwards
; happens on the HOST: run-libtest.sh converts the container back, extracts it, and one
; ctest case per program diffs its output against the same .expected file b6sim is held to.
;
; SO THERE IS ALMOST NO ASSERTION HERE, as in session.ini and for the same reason.  The
; markers below say only that each program ran to its end; whether it ran CORRECTLY is a
; question about twenty-seven files, and it is asked where it can be answered properly.
;
; WHY ONE expect PER PROGRAM rather than one big step.  A rule that fires HALTS the machine
; and its action restarts it, so the run is bounded by the ctest TIMEOUT instead of by a
; single step budget -- twenty-seven programs, forty-odd execs and a sleep(1) in `signals' do
; not fit comfortably in one.  It also turns a hang into a diagnosis: the last marker that
; appeared names the program that did not finish, and the next one names what to look at.
; The markers cannot fire early or out of turn -- each is a distinct string, a rule is
; removed once it has matched, and the programs' own output goes to files rather than to
; this stream, so there is nothing else here to match by accident.
;
; THE DISK IS A COPY, AND IS WRITABLE.  root3080.disk is converted out of root.img by
; run-libtest.sh, freshly for every run, with /etc/libtest grafted in; root3072.disk, the
; build artifact, is never written by anything.  Not `-r', since the whole run writes; not
; `-n' either, which would CREATE AND FORMAT an empty container on which sbcheck() fails.
; 3072 is the artifact, mdtest owns 3073-3076, session 3077, console 3078, and 3079 is the
; scratch volume kernel/test/CMakeLists.txt documents for a coninit bisection.
;
; THE DRUMS MUST BE ATTACHED TO EXEC ANYTHING -- they are swapdev, and exece() stages the
; argument list there before it touches the new image; with no drum every exec fails with
; EIO.  See boot.ini.in, which says it at length.  Named for this test because ctest runs
; the SIMH tests in parallel and each wants a swap area of its own.
;
; `set mmu cache' and `set tty25 raw' for the reasons boot.ini.in and console.ini give.
; `step', not `go': a hang must fail the run instead of hanging it.
;
; WHY THE SHELL IS TOLD TO READ THE SCRIPT: there is no `#!' on this system -- the shebang
; is a 4.xBSD invention, and neither getxfile() nor b6sim implements it -- so `sh /etc/libtest'
; is how a script runs, exactly as runcom() in cmd/init/init.c execs `sh /etc/rc'.
;
; AND THIS .ini IS GENERATED, like swap.ini and for the same reason: it asserts on three
; kernel counters and those are link-time addresses.  genboot.cmake substitutes them; the
; file is read out of the BUILD directory, not the source one.
;
; What the counters are for.  iomove() (kernel/rdwri.c) hands every user transfer to
; copyinb()/copyoutb() (kernel/ucopy.c), which peel the partial words at each end and give
; the whole-word middle to the machine-language copyin/copyout.  Whether that middle is ever
; REACHED is invisible from the outside -- a kernel that peeled all 3072 bytes of every block
; one at a time would produce identical output, only slowly -- so this run asserts that the
; bulk path carried something, exactly as swap.ini asserts that something was swapped.  That
; is also the only defence there is for `if (lead == NBPW) lead = 0;' in ucopy.c, which
; kernel/test/umem provably cannot see (its header says so).
;
; The other two are printed and not asserted, because they are a MEASUREMENT and not a
; contract: nioedge is what the peels cost (hundreds of bytes, against a million through the
; middle) and nioshift is what is left to win -- the transfers whose two pointers stand on
; different bytes of their words, which need a shifting copy this kernel does not have.

reset
set cpu latin
set mmu cache
set tty25 raw

attach tty25 console
attach md00 root3080.disk
attach -n drum0 libtest0.drum
attach -n drum1 libtest1.drum
load ../unix

expect "# " send "sh /etc/libtest\r"; step 1000000000
expect "ok hello"   step 1000000000
expect "ok vararg"  step 1000000000
expect "ok errno"   step 1000000000
expect "ok procs"   step 1000000000
expect "ok sbrkt"   step 1000000000
expect "ok malloct" step 1000000000
expect "ok strings" step 1000000000
expect "ok gen"     step 1000000000
expect "ok strtolt" step 1000000000
expect "ok environ" step 1000000000
expect "ok jmp"     step 1000000000
expect "ok headers" step 1000000000
expect "ok stdiot"  step 1000000000
expect "ok printft" step 1000000000
expect "ok scanft"  step 1000000000
expect "ok execs"   step 1000000000
expect "ok shellt"  step 1000000000
expect "ok memt"    step 1000000000
expect "ok suidt"   step 1000000000
expect "ok timet"   step 1000000000
expect "ok pwent"   step 1000000000
expect "ok signals" step 1000000000
expect "ok matht"   step 1000000000
expect "ok termcapt" step 1000000000
expect "ok cursest" step 1000000000
expect "ok puret"   step 1000000000
expect "ok curstty" step 1000000000
; The last rule does NOT step: it jumps to the checks below, which run with the machine
; stopped.  A `goto' rather than falling through, because falling through is also what a run
; that merely exhausted its step budget does, and the FAIL block exists to tell those apart.
expect "libtest done" goto counters

step 1000000000

echof "FAIL: the libtest run never finished. The last `ok <name>' above names the last program that COMPLETED, so the one after it in kernel/test/libtest.sh is the one that hung or died. Look the PC up in ../unix.nm for a kernel fault, or in build/lib/test/<name>.nm for a fault in the program. PC:"
ex PC
exit 1

:counters
if @NIOBULK@&07777777777777777==0 echof "FAIL: libtest -- iomove() never reached the bulk copy path, so every one of the million-odd bytes above went through the kernel one at a time.  The output would be identical; only the time would differ.  Either copyinb/copyoutb (kernel/ucopy.c) stopped squaring the pointers up to a word boundary, or nothing on the image does a transfer of six bytes or more any more.  niobulk:"; ex @NIOBULK@; exit 1

echof "PASS: libtest -- the shell ran all twenty-seven /usr/test programs off the image and called sync(2)"
echof "libtest: iomove bytes -- bulk, then the in-phase peels, then what is left out of phase"
ex @NIOBULK@
ex @NIOEDGE@
ex @NIOSHIFT@
quit
